  "Question": "The perimeter of one square is \\(3\\) times the perimeter of another square. The area of the larger square is how many times the area of the smaller square?",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(2\\)",
    "B": "\\(3\\)",
    "C": "\\(4\\)",
    "D": "\\(6\\)",
    "E": "\\(9\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "E",
  "Solution": "A square's perimeter is four times its side length. Dividing both perimeters by \\(4\\) therefore shows that the larger square's side is \\(3\\) times the smaller square's side.\n\nLet the smaller side length be \\(s>0\\). The smaller area is \\(s\\times s\\), while the larger area is\n\\[\n(3s)\\times(3s)=9(s\\times s).\n\\]\nThus the larger area is \\(\\boxed{9}\\) times the smaller area, choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{E}}\\).",
